# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cad-runner-wedged-by-modal-dialog — a CAD job that waits on an invisible dialog blocks every other CAD job
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-cad-runner-wedge

* Symptom

On 2026-08-14, with the Windows runner online for a limited window (laptop
on battery), NOT ONE CAD job ran in an hour. All of them sat =pending=.

The runner was online and idle-looking. What was actually happening:

: running: 1
:   verify:bricscad-hidden-ui:windows | pipeline 2761439584
:   started 21:12:16Z, duration 1937 s, status running

One job, running for 32 minutes, holding the =cad=-tagged runner — which is
configured =concurrency = 1= precisely so CAD jobs serialise. Everything
else queued behind it: 70 CAD jobs across nine pipelines by the time it was
noticed.

* Cause, in two layers

*The job hung* because it launched BricsCAD with neither a drawing nor a
profile. pjb: "sans dessins et sans profile, bricscad met des dialogues pour
demander a l'utilisateur quoi utiliser." Under =/Automation= the main frame
is hidden, so the dialog is invisible as well as unanswerable. Fixed
separately (=scripts/verify-bricscad-hidden-ui.ps1= now passes both).

*The job did not END*, and that is the part this ticket is about. alfe's own
=--timeout 180= fired — the READY wait gave up — but the CAD process alfe had
spawned stayed alive, so the job kept running. alfe's timeout protects the
PROTOCOL wait; it does not guarantee the engine is gone.

With no =timeout:= on the job, the ceiling was the project default (an
hour). One wedged job could therefore deny the shared CAD runner for an
hour, and did.

* Fixed here (partially)

=timeout: 30m= on the four CAD gate templates in =.gitlab/native.yml=. The
value is measured, not guessed: the longest legitimate CAD job observed is
=encoding:experiment:bricscad:windows= at 1158 s (19 min); every other CAD
job runs in under 223 s. 30m leaves ~55% headroom over the real outlier and
halves the worst case.

That caps the DAMAGE. It does not stop the wedge.

* Still open — the engine outliving its job

Two things worth doing, neither urgent now that the ceiling exists:

1. *alfe should reap the engine it spawned when the protocol wait times
   out.* It already does this on abnormal exit for BricsCAD (the .ps1
   drivers have a "safety net" that kills the exact PID alfe reported), which
   is evidence the problem was known but handled at the DRIVER level rather
   than in alfe. A READY timeout should terminate the launched process
   before returning, not leave it to whoever called alfe.
2. *A dialog-shaped hang deserves a distinct diagnosis.* "READY-TIMEOUT,
   last status BOOTING, launcher still running" is what BOTH a slow start
   and a modal prompt look like. If the engine is still alive and has
   produced no status transition at all, saying so — "the engine is running
   but has not moved past BOOTING; on Windows this is usually a startup
   dialog waiting for input" — would have pointed at the real cause in one
   read instead of three wrong hypotheses.

* Notes

The three wrong hypotheses are worth recording, since each looked plausible:
the default profile auto-loading the EPURE vertical application (refuted: the
launched argv carries no =/p= at all, and per pjb EPURE needs explicit
options); a missing document leaving =/b= no command context (plausible but
not the mechanism); and the =/Automation= switch itself being at fault
(refuted by the argv — the run never got far enough to exercise it).

Reading the command line the script already logs settled it in one look. It
is worth checking that first, before theorising about what a CAD does.

* PARTLY RESOLVED 2026-08-15 (clautolisp 1.8.39) — and the premise was wrong

The remaining item read "alfe should reap the engine it spawned when the
protocol wait times out". Reading the code first showed that BricsCAD
ALREADY DOES: START-ENGINE wraps the READY wait in an UNWIND-PROTECT that
terminates the engine unless the start completed. So the premise of this
half of the ticket was wrong, and stating it plainly matters more than
the code that follows.

What was actually missing, found by comparing the two backends:

1. *AutoCAD's START-ENGINE had no such guard at all.* On a READY timeout
   it signalled and left the spawned engine running. That is the exact
   shape of the incident, on the other backend.
2. *AutoCAD's SHUTDOWN used* =(uiop:terminate-process info)= *followed by
   an UNBOUNDED* =(uiop:wait-process info)=. That is precisely the call
   BricsCAD's own killer exists to avoid — its docstring says so: "can
   hang on Windows when the engine (a GUI BricsCAD) is slow to die or a
   child shares its handles -- that would freeze alfe in shutdown and
   hang the whole job."
3. *The careful killer was private to one backend.* %KILL-ENGINE-PROCESS
   lived in backend-bricscad.lisp, which is exactly how the other backend
   kept the dangerous call. Having one backend own the lesson is the
   structural defect behind both symptoms.

Fixed: KILL-ENGINE-PROCESS moves to backend-cad-common (it is not
BricsCAD-specific — AutoCAD spawns a GUI process the same way), AutoCAD's
shutdown uses it instead of the unbounded wait, and AutoCAD's START-ENGINE
gains the unwind-protect reaping, mirroring BricsCAD.

** Tests

Four, none needing a CAD:

- a live child is terminated;
- a child that TRAPS SIGTERM is still killed, and the call RETURNS
  promptly — the bounded-ness is the whole point, and a test that only
  killed a cooperative process would prove nothing;
- NIL and already-dead processes are tolerated, since the call runs from
  an unwind-protect on a failed start where the engine may never have
  been spawned;
- both backends use the bounded killer and neither issues
  =(uiop:wait-process info)= on the engine handle. Asserted on CODE with
  comments stripped: the comment explaining why that call was removed
  contains the call, and a check that its own rationale can fail is worse
  than no check. (It fired on exactly that during development.)

** RESOLVED 2026-09-27 (clautolisp 2.2.129) — the diagnosis half

"READY-TIMEOUT, last status BOOTING, launcher still running" now reads
differently in the case that matters.

=ENGINE-NEVER-STARTED-P= is the discriminator the message was missing:
=BOOTING= is what alfe ITSELF writes to status.txt before the launch, so
seeing it still there means the engine published nothing at all — it never
got as far as loading run-common.lsp. That is a different fact from a CAD
which reached =RUNNING= and stalled, and the two used to print the same
phrase.

=READY-TIMEOUT-DIAGNOSIS= turns it into three cases:

| launcher | engine moved? | phrase                                                    |
|----------+---------------+-----------------------------------------------------------|
| exited   | —             | =launcher exited with code N= (its output is quoted too)   |
| alive    | yes           | =launcher still running= — it started and stalled LATER    |
| alive    | no            | =never moved past BOOTING ... usually a startup dialog=    |

The third names the likely cause: BricsCAD asks which drawing and which
profile when given neither, and under a hidden main frame (=/Automation=)
that dialog is invisible as well as unanswerable — which is exactly the
2026-08-14 incident. On macOS the same shape is the Accessibility prompt
blocking the first =osascript= keystroke.

*AutoCAD's message had no launcher state at all* — found while wiring this,
and the same structural defect the earlier half of this ticket recorded: one
backend owning a lesson. Both now pass the last status and print the phrase.

Tests (=backend-cad-tests.lisp=), pure, no CAD needed:

- =ready-timeout-diagnosis-tells-a-dialog-from-a-slow-start= — the
  discriminating pair is the point: the SAME live launcher, one engine that
  never moved and one that did, must not produce the same phrase. Also that
  an exited launcher is NOT diagnosed as a dialog.
- =engine-never-started-p-is-exactly-the-no-transition-case= — nil, empty,
  =BOOTING=, lowercase, trailing newline and CRLF (what Windows writes) are
  all "never started"; =READY=, =RUNNING= and =FAILED= are all transitions.

alfe suite: *1316 checks, 0 failures, SBCL and CCL.* Documented in the alfe
user manual § Troubleshooting (with what to do about it, and the warning that
a cold start legitimately takes minutes) and in RELEASE_NOTES.org.

Nothing named in this ticket remains: the ceiling exists (=timeout: 30m=), the
engine is reaped by both backends through one bounded killer, and the
diagnosis now distinguishes the two shapes. Moved to =issues/closed/=.
